<p>CockroachDB can keep one relational database writable across machines and regions. That makes it a candidate when the application needs a failure boundary or data placement that a simpler deployment cannot meet. It also puts coordination, placement and client recovery inside the decision.</p>
<p>We will take the reservation service from <a href="/review/relational-next/choosing/">Choosing a relational database</a> into a conditional regional expansion. Follow one Irish customer reserving a mug, first from Irish stock and then from German stock. The result is a documented candidate screen and a concrete decision about what may proceed.</p>
<p><strong>No CockroachDB deployment was executed for this article.</strong> The SQL below is a proposed acceptance fixture, and every figure is an explanatory model. Primary documentation was checked on 20 October 2026. The distinction matters: a model can expose a failing design before money is spent, but cannot establish the production latency, correctness or recovery of an untested application.</p>

<nav aria-label="In this article"><ol>{sections.map(([id,title]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="name-the-requirement-that-needs-distribution">Name the requirement that needs distribution</h2>
<p>The parent’s shop accepts an entire region being unavailable. Under that brief, it postpones distributed SQL. Here the business changes one promise: customers served from surviving European regions must still reserve stock when any one database region is unavailable. Keep that change explicit; customers living in several countries do not by themselves require a distributed writer.</p>
<p>The stock counter and reservation still change in one transaction. Stock never becomes negative; a request has at most one reservation; retrying the same logical request cannot consume another mug. The request identity includes a fixed home region and a UUID, and its recorded original arguments must agree on replay. An Irish customer can deliberately request German stock, so a transaction crossing row homes is part of the contract.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="The conditional regional brief"><table><thead><tr><th>Inherited or changed promise</th><th>Acceptance boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Freshness</td><td>After a known successful reservation, its customer can retrieve that result through the supported surviving route. Reservation decisions and immediate confirmation reads use current transactional reads; no historical follower read substitutes for them.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Ordinary workload</td><td>Retain the parent’s 10 million reservations and roughly 100 GB initial data/index forecast, 200 reads and 20 reservation attempts/s, tenfold 60-second bursts with half the claims on one product. Measure each client region and local/cross-region stock separately.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Ordinary latency</td><td>At least 99% of responses within 300 ms from API entry, including acquisition, queues and retries; infrastructure errors below 0.1%. Sold-out is a valid business response.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>New region-loss gate</td><td>Lose no acknowledged reservation. From failure injection, restore usable reservation service for surviving clients within 60 seconds, then sustain the stated normal load with p99 within 600 ms. These proposed degraded targets need business approval before a real evaluation.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Other inherited gates</td><td>Approved European data, replica and backup locations; accidental-deletion recovery within one hour to a point at most five minutes before the mistake; £1,000/month recurring planning limit and four routine operator hours/month.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Traffic routing, row placement and failure survival are separate promises. An Irish SQL gateway can forward work to Germany. Irish row locality can still require copies elsewhere. A surviving database can remain unreachable if the only application gateway or credential service was in the failed region. Draw those dependencies before calling the system regional.</p>

<h2 id="turn-the-requirements-into-a-minimal-topology">Turn the requirements into a minimal topology</h2>
<p>The screened candidate is <strong>CockroachDB v26.3.2, self-hosted Enterprise on separate Linux amd64 virtual machines; no managed service tier</strong>. Its <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/releases/v26.3#v26-3-2">release notes</a> date that patch to 18 September 2026. The <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/releases/release-support-policy">support policy</a> lists v26.3 as an Innovation release, supported until 19 February 2027 with no Assistance Support phase. An upgrade rehearsal and an owner before that date are therefore adoption gates. This pin identifies the candidate examined here; it is not a recommendation to choose the newest numbered release.</p>
<p>The intended commercial arrangement requires an Enterprise licence and support quote. The <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/licensing-faqs">licensing documentation</a> distinguishes paid Enterprise, revenue-qualified Free, Trial and Evaluation licences, with renewal and telemetry conditions. A short development grace period is not a production entitlement. The <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/cockroachdb-software-license/">Software License</a> also has evaluation disclosure obligations. Establish eligibility, permitted use and ongoing cost with the organisation’s authorised owner before running or adopting this candidate. No licence or account was obtained here.</p>
<p>Use the smallest useful region set: Ireland, Germany and France, with node locality labels <code>eu-west-1</code>, <code>eu-central-1</code> and <code>eu-west-3</code>. These labels describe the proposed deployment; setting a flag does not place a machine there. Three regions are required by the documented <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/multiregion-survival-goals">region-survival goal</a>. For this rehearsal, choose three independent zones and one database host per zone in each region: nine hosts. This preserves a three-zone operating layout in every region and permits the zone checks as well as the region checks. Nine hosts is our chosen baseline, not a universal SQL minimum.</p>
<p>Each proposed host starts with eight vCPUs, 32 GiB RAM and a dedicated 256 GiB SSD-backed block volume, provisioned for at least 4,000 IOPS and 240 MB/s. These are starting resource choices, not measured capacity. The <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/recommended-production-settings">production checklist</a> supplies the hardware, independent-host, clock and network guidance to verify. Use certificate-verified TLS, restricted node connectivity, synchronised clocks, regional application endpoints and a bounded pool. The team owns those controls, patching, alerts and backups in this self-hosted arrangement.</p>
<Sql code={topology} label="Proposed database regions and survival goal; not a deployment transcript" />
<p>CockroachDB stores data in replicated portions of its key space called <strong>ranges</strong>. A voting replica participates in the majority needed to progress; a non-voting replica does not add a vote. A <strong>leaseholder</strong> coordinates access to a range. We need those distinctions only to predict this candidate’s failure and latency tests.</p>
<p>Figure 1 isolates one Irish range. Advance to losing Ireland, then switch to Zone survival at the same step. Watch which surviving copies can vote.</p>
<CandidateFigure scene="topology" number={1} />
<p>In the documented three-region region-survival arrangement, five voters spread 2+2+1 retain a majority after losing any one region. The extra protection costs remote write coordination. A cluster whose nodes merely carry three different region labels has not established this arrangement: wait for placement to converge and check every required range, including internal data, before injecting failure.</p>

<h3 id="place-each-transaction-s-data-and-work">Place each transaction’s data and work</h3>
<p>Use <code>REGIONAL BY ROW</code> for customers, reservations and warehouse stock when each row has a known home. Our small fixture makes the region explicit. A request’s home is part of its identity, not recalculated from whichever gateway happens to receive a retry. Inventory identity is the pair of warehouse region and SKU.</p>
<Sql code={schema} label="Proposed two-table fixture with explicit homes and a cross-home foreign key" />
<p>The <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/create-table#create-a-table-with-a-regional-by-row-locality-using-a-custom-region-column">custom region column</a> uses the database’s region enum. The composite request key enforces uniqueness within that fixed region; it does not establish a region-independent UUID namespace. If the business requires one global request identity independent of home, add and test the appropriate global enforcement and its coordination cost. Do not silently weaken uniqueness to make writes local.</p>
<p>The other locality choices answer different access patterns. <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/table-localities">Regional by table</a> gives a whole table one home; <code>GLOBAL</code> suits rarely changed reference data read from every region, with a write-latency cost. Stock is frequently updated, so making the entire inventory table global is not a free route to fast reservations everywhere.</p>
<p>Advance Figure 2 to the stock claim, then switch its warehouse. The API and reservation home stay Irish.</p>
<CandidateFigure scene="locality" number={2} />
<p>An Irish reservation of German stock must coordinate that stock decision with its Irish reservation. Customer foreign keys, uniqueness checks, secondary indexes and joins also belong on the path. For the full schema, record the home and index spans touched by each of those operations. A bounded history index can improve the query while still adding replicated write work.</p>
<Sql code={inspect} label="Evidence to collect on the candidate; output deliberately omitted" />
<p><a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/show-locality">SHOW LOCALITY</a> identifies the gateway. <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/show-ranges">SHOW RANGES</a> exposes replica and leaseholder locations; inspect indexes as well as table data. Its detailed mode gathers additional cross-node information, so use it deliberately. Retain the topology and placement snapshot with the workload run.</p>
<p>Locality optimises access; residency is a separate rejection gate. Inventory every physical replica, backup, diagnostic export and recovery copy against the approved locations. Even the documented <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/multiregion-overview#super-regions">super-region controls</a> have qualifications, including internal ranges and durability-driven placement. If a requirement prohibits the copies necessary for region survival, reject that arrangement rather than treating its home-region label as compliance evidence.</p>

<h2 id="check-the-application-s-actual-sql-contract">Check the application’s actual SQL contract</h2>
<p>PostgreSQL protocol support gets a driver connected. It does not establish equal types, expressions, locks or extension behaviour. In v26.3 CockroachDB advertises PostgreSQL 18 compatibility to clients; use <code>SELECT version()</code> for engine identity. The <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/postgresql-compatibility">compatibility reference</a> still lists substantive differences: range types are unsupported, integer division differs, and some session advisory-lock functions silently do no locking. An application relying on such a lock needs a replacement correctness argument, not just a successful function call.</p>
<p>For this shop, preserve exact decimal money, UUID identity and explicit region values through the selected application image. Pin its driver, ORM if any, pool mode, authentication and prepared-statement behaviour. Reuse <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/#preserve-values-and-authority-when-sending-sql">the value and authority checks</a> against CockroachDB; observations from the parent’s PostgreSQL server do not transfer automatically.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Candidate SQL and client acceptance set"><table><thead><tr><th>Real dependency</th><th>Small discriminating check</th><th>Decision if it fails</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Checks, foreign keys and identity</td><td>Reject negative stock, missing inventory, null amount and a duplicate fixed request key. Verify that a failed reservation insert rolls back its stock claim. Try the same UUID with different request arguments.</td><td>Reject a lost invariant; accept an adaptation only with its complete writer protocol and cost.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Value and SQL semantics</td><td>Round-trip a large integer, exact decimal, null, Unicode and timestamps through binding and response serialisation; test expressions and required extension operations from actual queries.</td><td>Document conversions or query changes; reject an indispensable unsupported feature.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Identifiers and driver state</td><td>Inspect generated defaults; prepare, reuse and invalidate statements; reconnect through another gateway; test rollback and pool reuse with the ordinary application role.</td><td>Change the dependency or schema, then repeat this same path.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Schema evolution</td><td>Build the history index and add a representative constraint with old and new application versions active; retain schema-job outcome, latency and rollback limits.</td><td>Defer adoption if the rollout cannot fit its operating budget.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Generated IDs deserve a separate observation. CockroachDB’s <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/serial">SERIAL translation</a> defaults to a generated 64-bit row ID, rather than assuming PostgreSQL sequence behaviour. Our logical request UUID is supplied before the attempt. It is not an ordering clock. If another application path requires sequences, verify its semantics and contention rather than replacing them incidentally during porting.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/online-schema-changes">Online schema change</a> is a facility with job, resource and coexistence constraints. It does not promise an arbitrarily large change has no application impact. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a> for the rollout method, and include region changes alongside regional-by-row index jobs when checking supported combinations.</p>

<h2 id="make-the-client-contract-survive-coordination-failures">Make the client contract survive coordination failures</h2>
<p>The candidate uses explicit <code>SERIALIZABLE</code> transactions. CockroachDB also documents <code>READ COMMITTED</code>; record the effective isolation instead of assuming an isolation name has PostgreSQL’s contract. Keep <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">the transaction article’s invariant argument</a> and test all stock-changing writers under the chosen protection.</p>
<Sql code={claim} label="The candidate stock claim; bind values on one owning client" />
<p>This illustrates the atomic claim, not the complete replay protocol. An empty insert result can mean no stock was claimed, including on a repeated request. A duplicate identity can roll back a claimed unit. Resolve a saved request and compare its arguments before interpreting those outcomes; arbitrate overlapping attempts through <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">the common request-recovery protocol</a>.</p>
<p>Some conflicts can be retried by the database internally. A surfaced <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/transaction-retry-error-reference">SQLSTATE 40001</a> requires the client’s supported transaction retry handling. The driver and application must agree on one retry owner. Repeat the transaction’s reads and decisions within the original deadline. Do not stack an ORM retry loop on top of an unaccounted driver loop or repeat an external payment from inside either.</p>
<p>A <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/common-errors#result-is-ambiguous">40003 ambiguous result</a>, or a disconnect around commit, is different: the application lacks a known outcome. At Figure 3’s second step, compare the two lost-reply cases. The application has the same evidence while the model’s database truth differs.</p>
<CandidateFigure scene="outcome" number={3} />
<p>In an authorised disposable candidate, first force a surfaced serialisation failure through the actual client; the documented <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/transaction-retry-error-example">retry-error example</a> can help make that path repeatable. Then test competing real reservations, a disconnect near commit and a deadline during hot-stock contention. Record error code, attempt count, cancellation, connection disposal and eventual resolved identity, alongside independent stock and reservation checks. Injected retry errors establish the error-handling path; they do not measure contention or prove a region-loss recovery.</p>

<h2 id="measure-locality-and-contention-with-production-shaped-work">Measure locality and contention with production-shaped work</h2>
<p>Use <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">the common workload comparison method</a> with four cases: local and cross-region stock, each with dispersed keys and one hot key. Keep schema, correctness, freshness and survival settings fixed. Run each client region separately and together against the forecast data volume, then doubled data, with reads, background work and schema or backup work that actually overlaps reservations.</p>
<p>Figure 4 is a deadline hypothesis. At the final step switch from dispersed stock to the hot row, then increase its network allowance. It adds queue and retry time to the original request.</p>
<CandidateFigure scene="budget" number={4} />
<p>The model explains why a fast ordinary attempt can coexist with a missed deadline during contention. Adding nodes can spread independent ranges; it cannot turn one authoritative stock row into independent decisions. Consider a changed stock-allocation design only if its semantics are acceptable, then test that as a new candidate. A key change that disperses inserts also does not automatically disperse a single popular SKU.</p>
<p>Retain completed useful throughput, p50/p95/p99 response time, infrastructure errors, sold-out outcomes, surfaced retries and final invariant results for each case. Count deadline failures in the acceptance population rather than dropping them from latency reporting. Capture CPU, storage, admission and connection waits over the same interval.</p>
<p>Use <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/explain-analyze">EXPLAIN ANALYZE</a> on safe representative work to inspect participating regions, scans and network work. It executes the statement. Use <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/monitor-and-analyze-transaction-contention">contention evidence</a> to identify waiting keys and transactions. A remote span supports a distance hypothesis; a hot inventory key supports a serialisation bottleneck; broad scans support a query-shape hypothesis. Change the smallest justified cause and repeat the same four cases. No vendor benchmark or local SQL run settles these gates.</p>

<h2 id="prove-the-named-failures-and-recovery-path">Prove the named failures and recovery path</h2>
<p>Before failure injection, wait for healthy replication and the required placement, including internal ranges, then record the exact build, settings and application routes. Keep an independent ledger of acknowledged request identities and original arguments outside the failed boundary. That ledger is how zero acknowledged loss becomes an observation.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Failures to distinguish in the candidate rehearsal"><table><thead><tr><th>Injected boundary</th><th>Evidence that decides the gate</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Client connection or SQL gateway</td><td>Observe rerouting, disposal of broken sessions and resolution of interrupted requests. A stable endpoint does not preserve a transaction’s connection.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>One node, then one whole zone</td><td>Verify which ranges retain quorum, service interruption, p99, errors and invariants while normal demand continues. Separate lost capacity from lost votes.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Each entire region, one at a time</td><td>From injection, observe usable surviving service within 60 seconds and the degraded 600 ms p99 gate. Reconcile every acknowledged identity and new successful write.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>A region isolated by network partition</td><td>The majority-connected side may progress; an isolated minority cannot make the same current-write promise. Check both client populations, reconnects and uncertainty instead of assuming an unreachable node is stopped.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Return and rebalance</td><td>Keep observing requests as copies and load move. Confirm healthy placement before the next fault; quorum arithmetic alone does not establish spare capacity.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>These are separate experiments in an isolated environment with explicit authority to inject each fault. A local set of processes sharing a laptop can check SQL and process behaviour, but shares one power, disk and network boundary. It cannot establish real region latency or regional independence. Provider restrictions that prevent an experiment remain recorded evidence gaps; an availability contract is contractual evidence, not its measured outcome. <a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/">Operating replicas and partitioned data</a> owns the general topology and operating procedure.</p>
<p>Replication preserves a committed deletion too. For accidental deletion, use CockroachDB’s <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/backup">BACKUP</a> and <a href="https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/v26.3/restore">RESTORE</a>, including revision history where point-in-time recovery needs it. The backup cadence, completion lag and retained history must jointly cover the five-minute recovery-point gate; merely scheduling every five minutes cannot guarantee it. Check external-storage access from the nodes, backup locations, independent credentials and recoverable encryption keys.</p>
<p>Rehearse the common <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">isolated restore and application validation</a> on representative volume. Include provisioning, retrieval, restoration, permissions, reconnection and reconciliation in the one-hour clock. Multi-region backup restore has topology restrictions; a convenient single-node target is not automatically a valid recovery destination. Preserve valid later orders when repairing a mistake, and prove the exit export/import path separately from operational backup.</p>

<h2 id="decide-whether-the-measured-contract-is-worth-its-cost">Decide whether the measured contract is worth its cost</h2>
<p>The proposed nine hosts total 72 vCPUs, 288 GiB memory and 2,304 GiB provisioned store capacity before backups. Losing one region leaves 48 vCPUs and 192 GiB memory while useful demand and repair work continue. Those are arithmetic capacity totals, not a throughput forecast. Size the surviving arrangement as deliberately as the healthy one.</p>
<p>Five voting copies of a 100 GB logical data-and-index footprint suggest about 500 GB before compression, retained versions, replication logs, other copies, metadata and free space; measure the actual stored footprint. Index entries are replicated too. Price baseline and degraded/replacement capacity, provisioned I/O, inter-region replication and query traffic, backups and retained history, restore storage, licences, support, monitoring and operator time. An unquoted Enterprise licence cannot be entered as £0.</p>
<p>This completed documentation screen yields the following record. “Deferred” means evidence is missing; “rejected” identifies an arrangement that already contradicts a gate.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="CockroachDB candidate decision record, 20 October 2026"><table><thead><tr><th>Gate and configuration</th><th>Evidence and result</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Why distribute?</td><td>The unchanged parent accepts region outage; the expansion introduces continued writes after a region loss.</td><td>Postpone this route under the original brief. Under the changed brief, retain it for evaluation.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Zone-only placement or two regions</td><td>Documented survival rules and the majority model do not establish survival of any one whole region.</td><td>Reject those arrangements for the new requirement.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>v26.3.2 Enterprise; three regions; nine independent hosts; region survival</td><td>Documented facilities support the proposed hypothesis. Licence/support quote, installed build, converged placement and upgrade rehearsal absent.</td><td>Defer adoption. Reassess release choice before the February 2027 support boundary.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>SQL/client and reservation correctness</td><td>Candidate fixture and discriminating cases defined. No CockroachDB SQL, driver, retry, disconnect or coexistence test was run.</td><td>Defer. Reject any indispensable unsupported feature or broken invariant; adaptation needs retesting.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Latency, capacity and region loss</td><td>No representative workload, regional network, fault injection or degraded-capacity result exists. Figure numbers are invented.</td><td>Defer. Require the stated ordinary and degraded gates; a good mean cannot offset a failing p99.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Residency, restore, exit and cost</td><td>Actual copy locations, timed restore, validated export/import and full quote/operator allocation remain absent.</td><td>Defer. Reject prohibited copies or a total exceeding the retained budget unless the business changes that gate.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>The decision is to keep the parent’s simpler route for its current brief and defer CockroachDB adoption for the proposed expansion.</strong> The next authorised evaluation would begin with commercial eligibility and release lifetime, then the compact SQL/client fixture, before paying for the regional workload and failure rehearsal. Passing each early screen earns the next expense; it does not grant production acceptance.</p>
<p>Once the evidence passes, record the actual driver versions, effective settings, locality rules, pool and deadline budgets, backup coverage, operator ownership and accepted cost as the starting configuration. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">the common change and cutover method</a> to establish a reversible enough entry and exit. Reassess after a release or tier change, a new region, changed locality or uniqueness, a hotter stock distribution, growth beyond tested capacity or any failure outside the rehearsed boundary.</p>

